Z||000000||fropuff||06-13-2001||12:19 PM||||Sorry, but I'm a little pissed right now.  On April 2, my mom was in an accident with a semi truck-trailer combo.  It totalled our truck and left $2500 worth of damage on his trailer.  Well, what happened was that it was a snowy day, and my mom was driving around a curve.  Well, after the curve, she saw the semi coming, but he was completely in his lane.  When she reached around 20 feet from him, he turned left in front of her.  It was snowy, and the ABS locked up, but she still went underneath the trailer.  She had no way that she could have stopped.  She was going only 20 MPH in a 30 zone, but it still totalled our '96 Silverado extended cab.  Well, we thought that since he saw our truck coming before he started making the turn, that it was his fault.  Well, I guess not.  The Minneapolis county court said that it was my mom's fault 100%.  We don't get anything, but we also said no to paying for his damage to the f**king trailer.  Dammit!!!<P>By the way, he also said, when he was reading the police report, that he had to wear glasses, which were a 275 prescription.  I also have this prescription, and I can't see a damn thing w/o lenses.  Also, since our truck is a 96, it has DRL (daytime running lamps), so they come on whenever the truck is on.  He had to see us.  Stupid bastard. <P>I'm done now...thanks.    <BR>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||06-13-2001||01:00 PM||||Sounds like some "under the table" funds changed hands. <BR>I'll bet the insurance company will still pursue it. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000002||DecadenceR||06-13-2001||02:17 PM||||He wasn't wearing his glasses? Or he was?<BR>If not and his license is endorsed for them, then I'd have a talk with a lawyer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-13-2001||02:35 PM||||you definately need to get a lawyer||||reg||1
Z||000004||kendall||06-13-2001||02:59 PM|||| Generally if car turning is hit in front of door pillar it is car turnings fault. If car turning is hit behind door pillar ( or in this case trailer) it is the person driving forwards fault. Hate to tell you but your mother is at fault, regardless of how fast, how slow, glasses on, glasses off, day lights, or no day lights the other vehicle was hit behind door pillar. ||||reg||8
Z||000005||fropuff||06-13-2001||03:14 PM||||Thanks for the replies.  <P>1) The insurance company didn't pursue it in the first place; the truck had liability and not full coverage.<P>2) He wasn't wearing glasses, but he had no mention of corrective lenses on his license; he had gotten them recently<P>3) Yep, it was hit behind the door pillar.  Where it hit was right behind the truck's rear wheels, the area of the fifth wheel.  That's why it took out the Silverado's A-pillar and twisted the frame.<P>4) I forgot to mention this...he had fifteen prior accidents on his record.  He got them in the first two years of driving a semi and none after that, until now that is.  <P>5) One more thing...the judge asked him what he was doing before he turned, and he said downshifting, as well as looking at the frickin gear shift.  Dumb ass.<P>6) U.S. laws suck ass.  <P>||||reg||8
